Here's a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML formatted text:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <sstream>

std::string markdownToHTML(const std::string& markdown) {
    std::string html;
    std::string line;
    std::istringstream ss(markdown);

    while (std::getline(ss, line)) {
        html += "<p>" + line + "</p>";
    }

    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "# Title\n\nThis is a first paragraph.\nThis is a second paragraph.\nThis is a third paragraph.";
    std::string html = markdownToHTML(markdown);
    std::cout << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

This function takes a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text as input and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. It uses a `std::istringstream` to read the markdown text line by line and wraps each line in an HTML `<p>` tag. The `main` function demonstrates how to use the `markdownToHTML` function and prints the resulting HTML text.

Note that this implementation assumes a simple markdown format and may not handle more complex markdown syntax or non-standard HTML tags.

